From 3ca3af6ee19ce823fd9fc179333e2b4b7827c488 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: valix <djvalic.wc@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:47:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] mips: jz47xx: x2600: SPL in the boot ROM's SRAM window,
 with a second try

The boot ROM loads the SPL image (2 KiB header, then the code) to
0x80001000 and starts it at 0x80001800; before the DDR is up only the
SRAM window up to 0x80007000 exists for it. The SPL had its BSS at
0x80007000, its heap at 0x80009000 and its stack at 0x8000D000, outside
the window, and with U-Boot's MMC, SDHCI and block layers its code alone
was 22 KiB of the 22 KiB there are.

- Link with a script of its own (x2600/u-boot-spl.lds): code, data, BSS
  and a 2 KiB stack reserve below the top of the window, checked at link
  time (and SPL_FOOTPRINT_LIMIT); the stack starts at 0x80007000; no heap.
- Read the eMMC with a small PIO reader (spl_emmc.c), the eMMC sequence
  of Ingenic's SPL driver for this SoC: about 2 KiB instead of 10. The
  image is now about 10 KiB, 24 blocks; GCC's call graph puts the
  deepest stack at 384 bytes.
- Pins as the stock SPL sets them: MSC0 on PD0-PD5 (and the SDK's SFC
  group PD6-PD11) function 0 without pulls, UART0 on PE9-PE12 function 1
  with the pull-up.
- Every CGU clock's parent as Ingenic's cgu_clks_set() sets them for
  this board's configuration, not only the DDR's.
- DDRC_AUTOSR_EN 0, as the stock SPL's parameter table has it.
- lpj=11935744 on the command line, as the stock SPL passes it.
- No guess whether the DDR is running: a separate build,
  CONFIG_AD5X_SPL_CHAINLOAD, leaves the clock and DDR setup out for
  chainload tests from Linux; it carries a marker that the image tools
  refuse.
- A second try instead of hang(): each stage runs with the watchdog
  armed (10 s) and records how far it got in the CPM scratch pad, which
  survives a watchdog reset; a failure resets through the watchdog. A
  load failure tries the other slot next; after three attempts the SPL
  stops with the watchdog off.
- U-Boot proper from eMMC 0xA400 when a U-Boot uImage with good header
  and data CRCs is there and it didn't fail to come up last time (the
  scratch pad says), with the watchdog armed for 30 s; otherwise the
  stock SPL's boot of the flagged slot, now with the uImage's header and
  data CRCs checked, and the watchdog stopped as the stock SPL leaves it.
- The watchdog code is shared by the SPL and U-Boot proper; no 64-bit
  division in it, and the SPL's delays come from the OST directly.
- start.S: no XBurst1 Config7 BTB write on the X2600, which the stock
  SPL doesn't do either.
